# What a digest costs, watched.
#
# sha256 is a streaming algorithm: it walks the padded message sixty-four
# bytes at a time, carrying eight state words, and everything a block builds —
# the sixty-four schedule words and the sixty-four intermediate states — is
# dead the moment the next block starts. So the arena should hold a constant
# amount however long the message is.
#
# It does, since 2026-08-31. It did not before, and this fixture was written
# to watch that: an 8,800-byte message read 713,523 allocations and 90,177,536
# arena bytes, an 88,000-byte one read 7,118,218 and 889,192,464 — ten times
# the input for ten times the memory, 849 MB of resident set to digest 88 KB —
# and `alloc_bytes` sat within one per cent of `arena_peak_bytes` at every
# size, which is one fact said twice: nothing was reclaimed between blocks, so
# the peak WAS the total. The arena now holds one 1 MiB block from 1 KB
# through 32 KB, four at 64 KB and 100 KB, and twelve at 200 KB — no longer a
# constant, and no longer ten thousand bytes an input byte either.
# `tests/sha256_peak.rs` pins the interpreter's figure at three sizes and
# `tests/a_program_is_not_its_directory.rs` pins the other half of it.
#
# What held it was the import line, and not for a reason anyone would guess
# from reading sha256. `beat.rs` decided which loops could rewind their arena
# by asking whether the declaration's source path began `std/` or `lib/` — the
# field error origins are built from, a path for a diagnostic read as a
# semantic marker — so every std module lost its carry, and so did any package
# an author happened to keep in a folder called `lib`. Removing the test costs
# 254 more allocations in 637,892 on an 8 KB message. The
# second and third log entries for 2026-08-31 have the bisection, the
# emitted-IR diff, and what the exclusion turned out to be protecting, which
# was nothing the classifier was not already refusing.
#
# THE ROWS BELOW ARE THE OTHER SIDE OF THAT TRADE, so they are worth reading
# as a cost rather than as a win. The message here is forty-four bytes — ONE
# padded block — because a fixture running at the sizes above would take a
# golden suite minutes, and at one block there is nothing to reclaim: the
# rewinds happen, evacuate the state words, and give back a segment the
# program was never going to outgrow. So allocations went 1,980 -> 5,259 and
# allocated bytes 424,369 -> 638,865 for a peak that did not move, and
# `thunk_evals` went 1 -> 64 because a memo whose result the rewind would
# reclaim is declined and recomputed. This is the small end of a curve whose
# large end is 86x; the fixture pins the constant and the two specs named
# above pin the slope.
#
# Watched, not frozen: regenerate deliberately and say which way it went.
import "std/sha256"
import "std/text"

message = "the quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog."

pub play = print (text/slice (sha256/hex (text/bytes message)) 1 16)
